## Nachtrag 2026-08-06 — entschieden, entblockt

Die Deferred-Marker heißen in Rust **`n_!`, `nn_!`, `np_!`, `nnp_!`, `nd_!`,
`ndn_!`, `ndp_!`, `ndnp_!`** (snake_case). Die `l*!`-Familie bleibt wie in den
anderen Runtimes.

Für den Scraper heißt das: `func_qr` muss für Typ `rs` **zwei** Muster kennen —
`l(?:n|p|np|d|dp|dn|dnp)?!` und `n(?:|n|p|np|d|dp|dn|dnp)_!`, also die N-Familie
kleingeschrieben. Das ist der zweite Grund für einen typabhängigen `func_qr`
(der erste ist Go mit `L`, `Ln`, …); heute ist es ein einziges `has func_qr`
mit festem Default.

`%arg_lists` in `required_args` ist nach Funktionsname geschlüsselt (`N_`,
`Nn_`, …). Mit drei Schreibweisen derselben Funktion (`N_` in Perl/Python/JS/PHP,
`N_` in Go, `n_` in Rust) muss der Lookup normalisieren, statt die Tabelle zu
verdreifachen — z.B. Funktionsname vor dem Nachschlagen auf die kanonische Form
abbilden.
Implemented. lib/Locale/Simple/Scraper.pm: --rs=s, push @rs,'rs', %e entry,
POD (OPTIONS + SUPPORTED CALLS, incl. the two accepted name-parity
departures and the (...)-only decision). No %f changes needed — rs
canonicalizes to the existing lowercase l*/uppercase N_* keys, same as
go's L*-folding already does.

Parser.pm, type rs:
- func_qr: two alternations, both requiring a trailing "!" — l(n|p|np|d|dp|dn|dnp)?!
  and n(|n|p|np|d|dp|dn|dnp)_! — no path-prefix branch needed for
  locale_simple::l!(...), ':' isn't \w so noise()'s substring_before
  already eats it, same mechanism as php's \ and go's . prefixes.
- canonical_func: strips the "!", then folds only the n_-family up via
  ucfirst (n_ -> N_, ndnp_ -> Ndnp_); l!/ldnp!/... are already canonical.
  Opposite direction from go's L->l fold, needed because rs's two
  families disagree on case within the same file.
- concat_op: rs => undef (explicit now, constant_string's undef-branch
  from #5 collapses to a single any_of literal, no concatenation chain).
- literal_forms: "..." (shared double_quote_string_contents) plus raw
  strings via new rust_raw_string, a bare-method-name entry (like php's
  heredoc/nowdoc) since the close pattern isn't static.
- rust_raw_string: matches r(#*)" , builds close = qr/"\Q$hashes\E/ from
  the captured hash count, reuses raw_string_contents(close), then
  expects the same close literally — this is the "dynamically built qr//"
  piece #5 anticipated but didn't implement. Verified by hand: r"...",
  r#"..."#, r##"...##"## all round-trip correctly, including embedded
  quotes and near-terminator sequences with fewer hashes than required.
  No single-quote branch at all (char literals + lifetimes both produce
  bare/unpaired apostrophes — confirmed via smoke test that `fn f<'a>`
  and `'x'` don't desync the parser). arguments()/op() untouched — only
  (...) supported, {...}/[...] documented as deliberately unsupported.

